INT. FAMILY LIVING ROOM - UPRIGHT PIANO - EVENING

A dim room. A lamp with a yellowed shade. A walnut upright against the wall, its front panel off, hammers exposed like a ribcage.

ARNOLD TEMPLETON-WU, 60s, piano tuner, kneels beside it with a tuning lever. His daughter JOSEFINA, 30s, leans in the doorway, coat still on.

She doesn't come in.

He strikes a key. Listens. Adjusts a pin. Strikes again.

JOSEFINA
You didn't call.

ARNOLD
I wrote.

JOSEFINA
A postcard isn't a call.

ARNOLD
It's quicker.

JOSEFINA
It took six weeks.

He strikes a key. Doesn't answer.

JOSEFINA (CONT'D)
Mom's piano.

ARNOLD
She'd want it tuned.

JOSEFINA
She'd want a lot of things.

He pauses. Rests the lever on the strings.

ARNOLD
Do you want to sit?

JOSEFINA
I'm fine.

ARNOLD
There's tea.

JOSEFINA
I'm fine.

He nods. Resumes. A key. A pin. A key.

She finally crosses the room. Drops her bag. Perches on the arm of the sofa.

JOSEFINA (CONT'D)
How long have you been working on it?

ARNOLD
Three days.

JOSEFINA
That's a lot for a tuning.

ARNOLD
It's an old instrument.

JOSEFINA
Dad.

ARNOLD
Hm.

JOSEFINA
You don't have to be here alone.

ARNOLD
I'm not alone. I've got the piano.

JOSEFINA
That's the saddest thing you've said all year.

He strikes a key. A beat. Another.

ARNOLD
This one's flat.

JOSEFINA
They all are.

ARNOLD
No. Just this one. Listen.

He plays an A. Then the A above it. They clash faintly. Josefina tilts her head.

JOSEFINA
I can hear it.

ARNOLD
That's your mother's ear.

JOSEFINA
She told me once she could hear when a room was out of tune.

ARNOLD
She could.

JOSEFINA
What did the room sound like when she left?

He stops. His hand hangs above the keys.

ARNOLD
Like this.

He plays the flat A. It hangs between them, wavering.

JOSEFINA
Can you fix it?

ARNOLD
I've tried.

JOSEFINA
Let me.

ARNOLD
You've never tuned.

JOSEFINA
I've listened my whole life.

He studies her. Hands her the lever. She kneels beside him. Presses the key. Turns the pin a hair, then another, her head cocked, her eyes closed.

The A rises. Settles. Becomes true.

She opens her eyes. He is watching her, not the piano.

ARNOLD (CONT'D)
That's it.

JOSEFINA
That's it?

ARNOLD
That's the note.

She plays it again. A clean, level tone. The room seems to lean toward it.

JOSEFINA
(quiet)
I should have come sooner.

ARNOLD
You came.

She sets down the lever. He puts a hand on the piano's side, the way you might touch a shoulder.

ARNOLD (CONT'D)
Want tea now?

JOSEFINA
Yes.

He rises, knees cracking. Goes to the kitchen. She stays at the piano and plays, one finger at a time, the first line of a lullaby.
